What we have built so far

You now have an Advanced Observability Lab on GCP with:

Two VMs in the same VPC

monitoring-vm → runs OpenTelemetry Collector (otelcol-contrib)

app-vm → runs a Node.js web app instrumented with OpenTelemetry SDK

Data flow (Traces path)

User hits the app:
curl http://app-vm:8080 → response: “Hello from Advanced OTEL Lab App!”

1 Node app generates traces via OpenTelemetry.

Traces are exported via OTLP HTTP to:
http://<monitoring-vm-private-ip>:4318/v1/traces

OpenTelemetry Collector on monitoring-vm receives the spans on 4317/4318.

Collector exports them (right now) to:

logging exporter → you saw in journalctl:

TracesExporter {"resource spans": 1, "spans": 10}


(Option configured for Google Cloud Trace – IAM is now fixed so the next step is to see traces in the console.)

 So: App → OTEL SDK → OTLP → Collector → Exporter is working end-to-end.

2 Real production use cases – where this pattern is used

This exact pattern is used in real systems for:

1. Microservices Request Tracing

When a request passes through API gateway → service A → service B → DB, you want:

One Trace ID across all services.

Span-level timings and errors.

You put OTEL SDK in each service and send everything to a central OTEL Collector (like your monitoring-vm).

Collector then sends traces to:

Cloud Trace / Jaeger / Tempo / X-Ray / Datadog / New Relic, etc.

2. Debugging Latency & Bottlenecks

If users say “App is slow”, traces will show:

Service time split: app logic vs DB vs external API.

Which endpoint is slow, which dependency is the culprit.

Your lab lets students:

Trigger load with for i in {1..20}; do curl ...; done

See burst of spans in Collector logs and (later) in Cloud Trace UI.

3. Standard Observability Pattern for Platform Teams

Platform/DevOps teams don’t want every app to talk directly to GCP/APM vendors.

Instead:

Apps send OTLP → central OTEL Collector (your monitoring-vm).

Collector handles:

Routing (different exporters)

Load shedding

Sampling

Protocol conversion.

Your lab is exactly that architecture, just mini version.

4. Foundation for AIOps / SRE Labs

Later you can:

Stream traces/metrics/logs into a Flask/AI webhook.

Use Gemini to classify incidents, suggest RCA, etc.

This current lab becomes Phase 0: Raw signals for AIOps.

3 Step-by-step – Full lab (replay from scratch)

I’ll write this in README style so you can copy into README.md later.

Architecture:
User → app-vm (Node + OTEL SDK) → monitoring-vm (OTEL Collector) → exporters (logging / Cloud Trace etc.)

STEP 0 – Prereqs

GCP Project: testadi-459816

VPC: default (or any VPC where both VMs are in the same subnet)

Cloud Shell enabled

STEP 1 – Create the two VMs

From Cloud Shell:

gcloud config set project testadi-459816

# 1) Monitoring VM – runs OpenTelemetry Collector
gcloud compute instances create monitoring-vm \
  --zone=asia-southeast1-a \
  --machine-type=e2-micro \
  --image-family=ubuntu-2204-lts \
  --image-project=ubuntu-os-cloud \
  --tags=monitoring-vm

# 2) App VM – runs Node.js sample app + OTEL SDK
gcloud compute instances create app-vm \
  --zone=asia-southeast1-a \
  --machine-type=e2-micro \
  --image-family=ubuntu-2204-lts \
  --image-project=ubuntu-os-cloud \
  --tags=app-vm


Both VMs are now in same zone + VPC and can talk over private IPs.

STEP 2 – Open firewall (HTTP for app, OTLP already on internal network)

You mainly need port 8080 for app demo from your laptop:

gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-app-http \
  --allow=tcp:8080 \
  --target-tags=app-vm \
  --description="Allow HTTP to app-vm on 8080"


(OTLP ports 4317/4318 are used inside VPC between VMs – you normally keep them private.)

STEP 3 – Install & configure OTEL Collector on monitoring-vm

SSH into monitoring-vm from Cloud Console or Cloud Shell:

gcloud compute ssh monitoring-vm --zone=asia-southeast1-a

3.1. Install otelcol-contrib and net-tools
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y net-tools ca-certificates curl gnupg lsb-release

# On Ubuntu 22.04, otelcol-contrib may already be in repo; if not, add vendor repo
sudo apt install -y otelcol-contrib

3.2. Edit OTEL config

Open the config:

sudo nano /etc/otelcol-contrib/config.yaml


Use a minimal working config like:

receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc:
      http:

exporters:
  logging:
    loglevel: info
  googlecloud:
    project: testadi-459816

service:
  pipelines:
    traces:
      receivers: [otlp]
      exporters: [logging, googlecloud]


Important: Do NOT add service.telemetry.feature-gates block – that caused the earlier “has invalid keys: feature-gates” error with v0.107.0.

Save & exit.

3.3. Restart Collector and validate ports
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart otelcol-contrib
sudo systemctl status otelcol-contrib

# Confirm OTLP ports are listening
sudo netstat -tulnp | grep 431


You should see:

tcp6  0  0 :::4317  :::*  LISTEN  <pid>/otelcol-contrib
tcp6  0  0 :::4318  :::*  LISTEN  <pid>/otelcol-contrib

3.4. Watch collector logs
sudo journalctl -u otelcol-contrib -f


You should see startup logs and later:

Everything is ready. Begin running and processing data.

STEP 4 – Give the VM service account permissions (for GCP exporters)

Back in Cloud Shell (or anywhere with gcloud):

Get the project number and compute default service account:

gcloud config set project testadi-459816
gcloud projects describe testadi-459816 --format="value(projectNumber)"
# Example: 541531337654

gcloud iam service-accounts list --filter="compute" --format="value(email)"
# Example: 541531337654-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com


Grant required roles:

gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding testadi-459816 \
  --member="serviceAccount:541531337654-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/cloudtrace.agent"

gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding testadi-459816 \
  --member="serviceAccount:541531337654-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/logging.logWriter"

gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding testadi-459816 \
  --member="serviceAccount:541531337654-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/monitoring.metricWriter"


This makes sure otelcol-contrib on monitoring-vm can push to Cloud Trace / Logging / Monitoring.

STEP 5 – Build the Node.js app with OTEL on app-vm

SSH to app-vm:

gcloud compute ssh app-vm --zone=asia-southeast1-a

5.1. Install Node.js & npm (if not already)

If Node 18 isn’t there:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y nodejs npm
node -v
npm -v

5.2. Create app folder & basic HTTP server
mkdir -p ~/observability-app
cd ~/observability-app

npm init -y

cat << 'EOF' > server.js
const http = require("http");

const PORT = 8080;

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === "/health") {
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
    return res.end("OK");
  }
  res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  res.end("Hello from Advanced OTEL Lab App!");
});

server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`App running on port ${PORT}`);
});
EOF


Quick test (without tracing):

node server.js
# In another terminal:
curl http://localhost:8080
# Expect: Hello from Advanced OTEL Lab App!
Ctrl+C to stop node

5.3. Install OpenTelemetry packages
cd ~/observability-app

npm install \
  @opentelemetry/sdk-node \
  @opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node \
  @opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http

5.4. Create tracing.js (final working version)

Now replace <MONITORING_VM_PRIVATE_IP> with your actual internal IP, e.g. 10.148.0.8.
(You can get it from gcloud compute instances describe monitoring-vm ... or the console.)

cd ~/observability-app

cat << 'EOF' > tracing.js
const { NodeSDK } = require("@opentelemetry/sdk-node");
const { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } = require("@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node");
const { OTLPTraceExporter } = require("@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http");

// IMPORTANT: replace this with your monitoring-vm INTERNAL IP
const OTEL_ENDPOINT = "http://10.148.0.8:4318/v1/traces";

const traceExporter = new OTLPTraceExporter({
  url: OTEL_ENDPOINT,
});

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  traceExporter,
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
});

// Note: NodeSDK.start() in this version does NOT return a promise; just call it.
sdk.start();

console.log("✔ OpenTelemetry tracing loaded (exporting to:", OTEL_ENDPOINT, ")");
EOF


Key fixes compared to your earlier errors:

No <...> around the IP – it’s a literal string URL.

No .then() chaining on sdk.start() → that’s why you got “Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'then')” earlier.

5.5. Run app with tracing enabled
cd ~/observability-app

# Kill any old node processes
pkill node || true

# Start app with OTEL preloaded
node -r ./tracing.js server.js


You should see:

✔ OpenTelemetry tracing loaded (exporting to: http://10.148.0.8:4318/v1/traces )
App running on port 8080


In another terminal on app-vm:

for i in {1..20}; do curl http://localhost:8080; sleep 1; done
# Expect: repeated "Hello from Advanced OTEL Lab App!"


From monitoring-vm (to test VPC-level connectivity):

curl http://<APP_VM_INTERNAL_IP>:8080
# Expect: Hello from Advanced OTEL Lab App!

STEP 6 – Verify traces in the Collector

On monitoring-vm:

sudo journalctl -u otelcol-contrib -f


You should now periodically see:

TracesExporter {"kind": "exporter", "data_type": "traces", "name": "logging", "resource spans": 1, "spans": 10}


This means:

Node app → sends spans via OTLP

Collector → receives them on 4318

Collector → exports them (at least to logging)

End-to-end Observability pipeline is working

